---
import Icon from "~/components/Icon.astro";
import Mark from "~/components/Mark.astro";
import MirrorDemo from "~/components/MirrorDemo.astro";
import Layout from "~/layouts/Layout.astro";

const link =
  "inline-flex items-center gap-0.5 text-[var(--text-primary)] hover:text-[var(--accent)] transition-colors";
const mutedLink = "inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-[var(--text-muted)] hover:text-[var(--accent)] transition-colors";
const section = "border-t border-[var(--border)] pt-10";
const heading = "font-medium text-[var(--text-primary)]";
---

<Layout>
  <section>
    <Mark class="h-10 w-10 text-[var(--text-primary)]" />
    <h1 class={`mt-3 ${heading}`}>Kamui</h1>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
      Use a remote machine's <code>localhost</code> from your laptop. If you <code>ssh foo</code> into a dev box, run
      <code>kamui foo</code> instead. Kamui watches which ports are listening on the remote machine and opens the same ports
      on your laptop, over the SSH you already use.
    </p>

    <div class="code-block mt-6">
      <pre class="pr-10"><span class="p">$ </span><span id="install-cmd">brew install narasaka/tap/kamui</span></pre>
      <button
        id="copy-install"
        type="button"
        aria-label="Copy install command"
        class="group absolute top-2 right-2 flex h-6 w-6 items-center justify-center bg-[var(--surface)] text-[var(--text-muted)] hover:text-[var(--text-primary)] data-[copied]:text-[var(--accent)] transition-colors cursor-pointer"
      >
        <Icon name="copy" class="h-3 w-3 group-data-[copied]:hidden" />
        <Icon name="check" class="h-3 w-3 hidden group-data-[copied]:block" />
      </button>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-6">
      <MirrorDemo />
    </div>

    <div class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap gap-x-4 gap-y-1">
      <a class={mutedLink} href="/docs/how-it-works">How it works<Icon name="arrow-right" class="h-3 w-3" /></a>
      <a class={mutedLink} href="/docs/cli">CLI<Icon name="arrow-right" class="h-3 w-3" /></a>
      <a class={mutedLink} href="https://github.com/narasaka/kamui" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >GitHub<Icon name="arrow-up-right" class="h-3 w-3" /></a
      >
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class={section}>
    <h2 class={heading}>Why</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-3 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
      <p>
        I do my development on an always-on remote machine, so agents keep working when I close my laptop or lose Wi-Fi.
        The browser was the problem. An agent would start a dev server and print <code>http://localhost:3000</code>, which
        meant the remote machine in its terminal and my laptop in my browser.
      </p>
      <p>
        I kept a growing list of <code>LocalForward</code> lines to paper over it. Every new port meant editing that list and
        reconnecting, on every laptop. MCP sign-ins broke it completely: the provider redirects to
        <code>http://localhost:&lt;port&gt;/callback</code>, a random port the agent opened a moment ago. No list of forwards
        can cover a port that changes every time.
      </p>
      <p>
        Kamui finds the ports for me, including those short-lived callback ports. It uses the SSH setup I already have,
        keeps remote services bound to loopback, and installs nothing on the remote side.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class={section}>
    <h2 class={heading}>How it works</h2>
    <ul class="mt-6 space-y-6">
      <li>
        <p class={heading}>Your SSH, as is</p>
        <p class="mt-1 text-[var(--text-muted)]">
          <code>kamui foo</code> hands <code>foo</code> to the system OpenSSH client. Aliases, jump hosts, keys, and Tailscale
          names all work, with normal host-key checks.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class={heading}>Ports show up within a second</p>
        <p class="mt-1 text-[var(--text-muted)]">
          One discovery command lists the remote's listening TCP ports every second with <code>ss</code>,
          <code>lsof</code>, or <code>netstat</code>. Each one opens on your laptop's <code>127.0.0.1</code> and
          <code>::1</code> and goes away when the remote service stops.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class={heading}>OAuth callbacks just work</p>
        <p class="mt-1 text-[var(--text-muted)]">
          A callback port is mirrored before you finish signing in, so the redirect to <code>localhost</code> reaches the
          agent instead of your laptop.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class={heading}>Polite about conflicts</p>
        <p class="mt-1 text-[var(--text-muted)]">
          A port already taken on your laptop stays with whoever has it. Ports under 1024 are opt-in. Nothing touches proxy
          settings, firewall rules, or network extensions.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <a class={`mt-6 ${mutedLink}`} href="/docs/how-it-works">The details<Icon name="arrow-right" class="h-3 w-3" /></a>
  </section>

  <section class={section}>
    <h2 class={heading}>Usage</h2>
    <div class="code-block mt-6">
      <pre><span class="p">$ </span>kamui foo
DESTINATION  STATE
foo          <span class="ok">● connected</span>

MIRRORED:     3000, 5173, 5432
EXCLUDED:     22
CONFLICTS:    -
MIRROR ERROR: -

To disconnect, run: kamui stop foo</pre>
    </div>
    <div class="code-block mt-3">
      <pre><span class="c"># sessions, ports, conflicts</span>
<span class="p">$ </span>kamui status
<span class="c"># ports under 1024 are opt-in</span>
<span class="p">$ </span>kamui foo --include-port 443
<span class="c"># check your setup</span>
<span class="p">$ </span>kamui doctor foo
<span class="c"># or: kamui stop --all</span>
<span class="p">$ </span>kamui stop foo</pre>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--text-muted)]">
      The destination can be an alias from <code>~/.ssh/config</code>, a hostname, or <code>user@host</code>. Start your
      services the way you always do. <a class={link} href="/docs/cli">All commands<Icon name="arrow-right" class="h-2.5 w-2.5" /></a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class={section}>
    <h2 class={heading}>Install</h2>
    <div class="code-block mt-6">
      <pre><span class="c"># CLI</span>
<span class="p">$ </span>brew install narasaka/tap/kamui
<span class="c"># menu bar app + CLI</span>
<span class="p">$ </span>brew install --cask narasaka/tap/kamui-app
<span class="c"># from source</span>
<span class="p">$ </span>go install github.com/narasaka/kamui/cmd/kamui@latest</pre>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--text-muted)]">
      Runs on macOS and Linux, on either end. You need OpenSSH locally. The remote needs an SSH server with TCP forwarding
      and one of <code>ss</code>, <code>lsof</code>, or <code>netstat</code>.
      <a class={link} href="/docs/install">More<Icon name="arrow-right" class="h-2.5 w-2.5" /></a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class={section}>
    <h2 class={heading}>Menu bar app</h2>
    <p class="mt-6 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
      On macOS 13 or later, the menu bar app lists your sessions and their ports, opens a port in your browser with one
      click, and connects to hosts from your SSH config. It drives the <code>kamui</code> CLI, so sessions keep running if
      you quit it. <a class={link} href="/docs/menu-bar-app">More<Icon name="arrow-right" class="h-2.5 w-2.5" /></a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class={section}>
    <h2 class={heading}>Dedicated browser</h2>
    <p class="mt-6 text-[var(--text-secondary)]">
      <code>kamui browser foo</code> opens an isolated browser profile whose <code>localhost</code> points at the remote
      machine. It doesn't open ports for other programs on your laptop.
      <a class={link} href="/docs/how-it-works#the-dedicated-browser">More<Icon name="arrow-right" class="h-2.5 w-2.5" /></a>
    </p>
    <p class="mt-6 flex gap-2 text-[var(--text-muted)]">
      <Icon name="triangle-alert" class="mt-1 h-3 w-3 shrink-0" />
      <span>
        Every process on your laptop can reach mirrored services, and browsers treat them as <code>localhost</code>. Only use
        Kamui with hosts you trust.
      </span>
    </p>
  </section>

  <footer class="border-t border-[var(--border)] pt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-x-4 gap-y-1 text-[var(--text-muted)]">
    <a class="inline-flex items-center gap-0.5 hover:text-[var(--text-primary)] transition-colors" href="/docs">Docs</a>
    <a
      class="inline-flex items-center gap-0.5 hover:text-[var(--text-primary)] transition-colors"
      href="https://github.com/narasaka/kamui"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub<Icon name="arrow-up-right" class="h-3 w-3" /></a
    >
    <a
      class="inline-flex items-center gap-0.5 hover:text-[var(--text-primary)] transition-colors"
      href="https://github.com/narasaka/kamui/releases"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer">Releases<Icon name="arrow-up-right" class="h-3 w-3" /></a
    >
    <span class="ml-auto">MIT · <a class="hover:text-[var(--text-primary)] transition-colors" href="https://narasaka.dev">narasaka.dev</a></span>
  </footer>
</Layout>

<script>
  const button = document.getElementById("copy-install");
  button?.addEventListener("click", async () => {
    const text = document.getElementById("install-cmd")?.textContent ?? "";
    try {
      await navigator.clipboard.writeText(text);
    } catch {
      return;
    }
    button.setAttribute("data-copied", "");
    setTimeout(() => button.removeAttribute("data-copied"), 1500);
  });
</script>
